    qs:[
      { q:`How are elements primarily arranged on the periodic table?`, ch:[`Alphabetically`,`By increasing atomic number (number of protons)`,`By discovery date`,`Randomly, with no organizing principle`], ans:1, exp:`The modern periodic table arranges elements in order of increasing atomic number, which also groups elements with similar chemical properties into columns.` },
      { q:`What do elements in the same column (group) of the periodic table generally share?`, ch:[`The same atomic mass`,`Similar chemical properties, due to having the same number of valence electrons`,`Identical physical appearance`,`Nothing in common at all`], ans:1, exp:`Elements in the same group (vertical column) have the same number of valence (outermost) electrons, which gives them similar chemical reactivity and bonding behavior.` },
      { q:`What is a "period" on the periodic table?`, ch:[`A vertical column of elements`,`A horizontal row of elements`,`A single element's isotopes`,`A group of noble gases only`], ans:1, exp:`A period is a horizontal row on the periodic table; elements across a period have the same number of electron shells but differ in valence electrons.` },
      { q:`What general trend in atomic size occurs moving left to right across a period?`, ch:[`Atomic size generally increases`,`Atomic size generally decreases`,`Atomic size stays exactly constant`,`There is no consistent trend`], ans:1, exp:`Moving left to right across a period, atomic radius generally decreases because protons increase, pulling electrons in the same shell closer to the nucleus.` },
      { q:`Where are the most reactive metals typically located on the periodic table?`, ch:[`Far right side`,`Far left side (like Group 1, the alkali metals)`,`Exactly in the middle`,`Reactivity has no relation to position`], ans:1, exp:`The alkali metals (Group 1), on the far left of the table, are highly reactive because they have just one valence electron they readily lose to form a stable configuration.` },
    ],
