    qs:[
      { q:`Which general property is typical of metals?`, ch:[`Brittle and poor conductors of electricity`,`Shiny, malleable, and good conductors of heat and electricity`,`Always gases at room temperature`,`Never able to form bonds with other elements`], ans:1, exp:`Metals are typically shiny (lustrous), malleable (can be hammered into shapes), and good conductors of both heat and electricity.` },
      { q:`Where are nonmetals generally located on the periodic table?`, ch:[`On the far left side`,`On the upper right side`,`Scattered randomly with no pattern`,`Only in the middle rows`], ans:1, exp:`Nonmetals are mostly clustered in the upper right portion of the periodic table (with hydrogen as a notable exception on the left).` },
      { q:`What are metalloids?`, ch:[`Elements that behave exactly like typical metals`,`Elements with properties intermediate between metals and nonmetals, found along the "staircase" line on the periodic table`,`A synonym for noble gases`,`Elements that don't actually exist`], ans:1, exp:`Metalloids (like silicon and boron) sit along the zigzag line dividing metals and nonmetals, and show a mix of metallic and nonmetallic properties.` },
      { q:`Which of these elements is a well-known metalloid used in computer chips?`, ch:[`Oxygen`,`Silicon`,`Iron`,`Helium`], ans:1, exp:`Silicon is a metalloid whose semiconductor properties (partial electrical conductivity) make it essential for computer chips and electronics.` },
      { q:`Why are metals generally good electrical conductors?`, ch:[`Their electrons are tightly bound and don't move`,`They have loosely held valence electrons that can move freely, carrying electric current`,`They contain no electrons at all`,`Conductivity has nothing to do with electron behavior`], ans:1, exp:`Metal atoms hold their outer (valence) electrons loosely, allowing those electrons to move relatively freely through the material — this mobility is what allows metals to conduct electricity well.` },
    ],
